@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the searches of the chaos-magical run of 2026-10-22
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason given in prima-materia#703.
# The run closes the gap left by pmp:TyphonianMicrotubulusSearch: the authors
# that search named as not searched, and the physicists beside them. Four
# searches: the microtubule mapping across every reachable work, the place
# the quantum is actually put, the receiving organ in the two Typhonian
# volumes not read before, and the works that could not be reached.
#

pmp:ChaosMagicMicrotubulusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether any chaos-magical, Typhonian or adjacent work maps a receiver model onto neural microtubules"@en, "Ob irgendein chaosmagisches, typhonisches oder benachbartes Werk ein Empfängermodell auf neuronale Mikrotubuli abbildet"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Chaos magic: Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut, Liber Kaos (two further text layers), PsyberMagick (an upload without imprint), The Apophenion, The Octavo (Roundworld Edition 2011) and the essay Chaoism and Chaos Magic; Hine, Condensed Chaos, Prime Chaos, The Pseudonomicon (2004), Aspects of Evocation, Techniques of Modern Shamanism I-III and the French Chaos prêt-à-cuire; Fries, Visual Magick, Seidways, Kali Kaula; Sherwin, The Book of Results; Mace, Stealing the Fire from Heaven; Frater U.D., Practical Sigil Magic; Chapman, Advanced Magick for Beginners; White, The Chaos Protocols (2016); Vitimus, Hands-On Chaos Magic (three text layers); Wilde, The Grimoire of Chaos Magick; Dukes, How to See Fairies (2011); two anonymous chaos-magic tracts. Typhonian and adjacent: Grant, the seven volumes of the first search again and Hecate's Fountain (1992) and Against the Light (1997); Bertiaux, The Voudon Gnostic Workbook, Monastery of the Seven Rays year one, La Couleuvre Noire; Chumbley, the Dragon-Book of Essex (Grimorium Synomosia) and the Qutub commentary; Karlsson, Qabalah, Qliphoth and Goetic Magic; Webb, The Seven Faces of Darkness; Mason, Tunnels of Set. Exo-psychology: Leary, Exo-Psychology (1977), Info-Psychology (1987), Neurologic in two scans; Wilson, Cosmic Trigger I, Prometheus Rising, Quantum Psychology (1990). The physicists: Sarfatti and Herbert in Mondo 2000, issues 1 to 8 and 14 (1989-1995), and two issues of the Grey Lodge Occult Review. Secondary: Levenda, The Dark Lord (2013); Evans, The History of British Magic after Crowley (2007). All full-text layers at the open scan archive, searched whole."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which a chaos-magical, Typhonian or adjacent author maps contact with non-human intelligence, or any receiver model, onto the quantum properties of neural microtubules, or names the orchestrated objective reduction theory of Penrose and Hameroff; the second clause of the sentence held at prima-materia#888."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search, case-insensitive, over each text layer after folding runs of white space to one and joining words hyphenated at the line end, for: microtubule and its forms, tubulin, Hameroff, Penrose, orchestrated objective reduction, Orch OR, objective reduction, quantum, nervous system, antenna, receiver. Microtubule, tubulin, Hameroff and the theory: zero in every work. Penrose: three in The Apophenion (a sentence on time, the bibliography, the index) and one in Mondo 2000, issue 8, where Sarfatti names The Emperor's New Mind for a Platonic reality of mind. Quantum: high counts in The Apophenion, The Octavo, Quantum Psychology, Prometheus Rising, Info-Psychology and Exo-Psychology, low or zero in the rest; every co-occurrence of quantum with brain, neurone, synapse or nervous system within some two hundred and fifty characters was read in place. Positive control: the same pattern over a bundle of Portuguese translations of popular physics books returned twenty-six hits for Hameroff and five for the microtubule, so a text layer of this kind does yield the names."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, across some sixty text layers and every author the first search named as unsearched that could be reached. What the corpus has in the place of the microtubule is recorded by the next search: a quantum model of the brain, put into the brain as a whole, into states of it, or into the synapse, from Wilson in 1983 to Carroll in 2011, and no work of it names the theory the sentence attributes to it."@en ;
    skos:note "A finding about the tool, and it changes nothing above. The first count of this run, taken without folding white space, returned zero for nervous system in Info-Psychology, where the term stands one hundred and seventy-eight times between double spaces; the counts above are the folded ones. The earlier count of pmp:TyphonianNervusAntennaSearch, four for the seven Grant volumes, was repeated with folding and stands."@en .

pmp:ChaosMagicLocusQuanticiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Where the chaos-magical and exo-psychological corpus puts the quantum event in the brain"@en, "Wo der chaosmagische und exo-psychologische Bestand das Quantenereignis im Gehirn verortet"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The works of the preceding search in which quantum and brain, neurone, synapse or nervous system occur together: Carroll, The Apophenion and The Octavo; Leary, Exo-Psychology and Info-Psychology; Wilson, Prometheus Rising and Quantum Psychology; Sarfatti in Mondo 2000, issue 8."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The negation of the second clause of prima-materia#888 in its constructive form: not whether the microtubule is named, but where these authors place the quantum event when they place it in the body, and which physicists they name for it."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Every passage in which quantum occurs within some two hundred and fifty characters of brain, neurone, synapse or nervous system, read in place; the physicists' names Bohm, Walker, Sarfatti, Bell, Penrose, Hameroff, Herbert, Wolf counted in the same works."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:ChaosMagicCerebrumQuanticum, pmc:ChaosMagicCohaerentiaNeurologica, pmc:ExoPsychologyTransceptorNervosus, pmc:ExoPsychologyCircuitusQuanticus ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "Four places. Leary in 1977 makes the nervous system a transceiver of astrophysical signals and in 1987 rewrites the sender as quantum-physical; Wilson in 1983 gives the brain a non-local quantum circuit after Bohm, Walker, Sarfatti and Bell; Carroll in 2008 makes the brain a chaotic analogue quantum computer and in 2011 puts the damping of quantum thought into the chemical synapse. Quantum Psychology parallels the laws of the subatomic world with those of the nervous system and places nothing in it. The model the sentence describes is therefore there, in three authors and over thirty years, and its one specific element is not: the sentence has replaced the synapse and the brain at large with the best-known structure of a later theory."@en .

pmp:TyphonianNervusAntennaSearchSecunda a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the two Typhonian volumes not read before name the nervous system as the receiving organ"@en, "Ob die beiden zuvor nicht gelesenen typhonischen Bände das Nervensystem als empfangendes Organ nennen"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Grant, Hecate's Fountain (London: Skoob, 1992), in a full-text layer of the first edition; Grant, Against the Light (1997); searched whole by term, Hecate's Fountain read at the wording at every hit for extraterrestrial and for the receiving instance."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "The first clause of prima-materia#888 in the volumes the first search could not reach or did not include: a passage making the nervous system, the brain or a physiological organ the receiver of extraterrestrial forces; and, as its negation, a passage placing the receiver elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "As in the first search, with white space folded: nervous system, nerve, brain, antenna, receiver, receiving, wireless, radio, extraterrestrial and extra-terrestrial. Hecate's Fountain: nervous system zero; antenna four, read in place: twice a beetle and once a masked dancer in ritual narratives, and once the supreme antenna of Part Three, chap. 4, a pair of forces in the mind-will credited to Bertiaux; extraterrestrial fifty-six, read in place in the introduction and in Part Two. Against the Light: nervous system zero, antenna one."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TyphonianSemenNuitis ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "The volume that reads the Book of the Law for its extraterrestrial provenance names no organ at all; where it names the receiver, it is an entranced priestess, a priestess at the centre of a mandala, a dream state, and once an antenna made of two invoked forces in the mind. The work identifier carried the note that no full text had been found the day before; the scan lay in the same archive under a title naming the year 1993, which is the case of the access table in AGENTS.md about another identifier of the same work."@en .

pmp:ChaosMagicOperaInaccessaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the remaining works the first search named can be reached as full text"@en, "Ob die übrigen Werke, die die erste Suche nannte, als Volltext erreichbar sind"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Starfire journal, all volumes; Staley's essays; Dukes, SSOTBME and Thundersqueak; Carroll, Epoch (2014); the library scans of Carroll, PsyberMagick, and of Hine, Prime Chaos and The Pseudonomicon; Hine, Aspects of Tantra; Wolf, Space-Time and Beyond, Taking the Quantum Leap, The Eagle's Quest, The Dreaming Universe, The Spiritual Universe, Mind into Matter; Herbert, Faster than Light, Elemental Mind; Wilson, Cosmic Trigger III (1995); and, of the works of pmp:TyphonianOperaInaccessaSearch, Aleister Crowley and the Hidden God and Hecate's Fountain, the second of which was found and is recorded in the search before this one."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A readable full-text layer of each work, in any edition, so that the search for the microtubule mapping could cover it."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "The open scan archive searched by title, by author in both name orders and by free text, on 2026-10-22. Found under a lending restriction with the full text refused: the Starfire compilation of 2009, Epoch, the library scans of PsyberMagick, Prime Chaos, The Pseudonomicon and Hands-On Chaos Magic, all six works of Wolf, both of Herbert, Cosmic Trigger III; of these, PsyberMagick, Prime Chaos, The Pseudonomicon and Hands-On Chaos Magic lay a second time without restriction and were searched. Found and unreadable: Aspects of Tantra, whose text layer is the English print read through the Devanagari model, ASCII letter share zero. Found nothing: the Starfire volumes singly, Staley, SSOTBME, Thundersqueak. Not tried: other archives, a library with its own full-text access, print."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "The gap that remains for the second clause of prima-materia#888 is this list, and the one corpus in it that the issue named as plausible is the Starfire journal, the Typhonian house organ edited by Staley. It is in copyright and in print; the block is at the works and not at the route. Whoever holds the volumes can close it by a term search for the microtubule and Hameroff. Wolf and Herbert are physicists and not chaos magicians or Typhonians, and stand here because the first search named them."@en .
